
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Block Level and Inline Elements Assignment</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>HTML Block-Level and Inline Elements</h1>

    <p>
        HTML elements are mainly classified into two types:
        Block-Level Elements and Inline Elements.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <!-- ================================= -->
    <!-- 1. BLOCK LEVEL ELEMENTS -->
    <!-- ================================= -->

    <h2>1. Block-Level Elements</h2>

    <p>
        Block-level elements always start on a new line
        and occupy the available full width of their parent container.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 1: Heading Elements</h3>

    <h1>This is Heading 1</h1>
    <h2>This is Heading 2</h2>
    <h3>This is Heading 3</h3>

    <h3>Example 2: Paragraph Element</h3>

    <p>This is the first paragraph.</p>
    <p>This is the second paragraph.</p>

    <h3>Example 3: Division Element</h3>

    <div>
        This is the first division element.
    </div>

    <div>
        This is the second division element.
    </div>

    <h3>Example 4: List Elements</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>HTML</li>
        <li>CSS</li>
        <li>JavaScript</li>
    </ul>

    <ol>
        <li>Learn HTML</li>
        <li>Learn CSS</li>
        <li>Learn JavaScript</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Example 5: Other Block-Level Elements</h3>

    <blockquote>
        This is a blockquote element.
        It is used to represent a quotation.
    </blockquote>

    <pre>
This is a preformatted text element.
It preserves spaces and line breaks.
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <!-- ================================= -->
    <!-- 2. INLINE ELEMENTS -->
    <!-- ================================= -->

    <h2>2. Inline Elements</h2>

    <p>
        Inline elements do not start on a new line.
        They occupy only the space required by their content.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 1: Bold and Italic</h3>

    <p>
        This is <b>Bold Text</b> inside a paragraph.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is <i>Italic Text</i> inside a paragraph.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 2: Strong and Emphasis</h3>

    <p>
        This is <strong>Important Text</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is <em>Emphasized Text</em>.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 3: Underline and Strike</h3>

    <p>
        This is <u>Underlined Text</u>.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is <del>Deleted Text</del>.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 4: Span Element</h3>

    <p>
        HTML is <span>very interesting</span> to learn.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 5: Link Element</h3>

    <p>
        Visit
        <a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>
        to search for information.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 6: Image Element</h3>

    <p>
        This is an inline image:
        <img src="https://placehold.co/100x60" alt="Sample image">
        inside a paragraph.
    </p>

    <h3>Example 7: Other Inline Elements</h3>

    <p>
        Water formula: H<sub>2</sub>O
    </p>

    <p>
        Mathematical expression: X<sup>2</sup>
    </p>

    <p>
        Press <code>Ctrl + C</code> to copy.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <!-- ================================= -->
    <!-- 3. COMPARISON -->
    <!-- ================================= -->

    <h2>3. Difference Between Block-Level and Inline Elements</h2>

    <h3>Block-Level Example</h3>

    <div>First Block Element</div>
    <div>Second Block Element</div>
    <div>Third Block Element</div>

    <h3>Inline Example</h3>

    <span>First Inline Element</span>
    <span>Second Inline Element</span>
    <span>Third Inline Element</span>

    <hr>

    <!-- ================================= -->
    <!-- 4. COMBINING BOTH ELEMENTS -->
    <!-- ================================= -->

    <h2>4. Combining Block-Level and Inline Elements</h2>

    <div>
        <h3>Welcome to HTML</h3>

        <p>
            HTML is used to create
            <strong>web pages</strong>
            using different elements.
        </p>

        <p>
            You can learn more about HTML by visiting
            <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML">
                MDN Web Docs
            </a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <h2>5. Conclusion</h2>

    <p>
        Block-level elements start on a new line and generally
        occupy the full available width.
    </p>

    <p>
        Inline elements stay within the same line and occupy
        only the space required by their content.
    </p>

</body>
</html>
